On a \code{dtplyr} step, \code{\link[=nest_with_margins]{nest_with_margins()}} builds a lazy step and returns
it unexecuted; no row is processed until you collect it. To recover the
types and factor levels of the step's columns, marginplyr may evaluate a
zero-row version of its sources. A derived step whose types cannot be found
that way is refused; call \code{\link[dplyr:collect]{dplyr::collect()}} on it explicitly and pass the
data frame.
}

\section{Fixed columns and grouping dimensions}{

\code{.by} marks columns that are present in every grouping set, while
\code{.grouping} describes dimensions that can be omitted to form margins.
Columns in \code{.by} retain their input types and never receive
\code{.margin_label}.

